WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.820
Lactose-free milk starts
with an enzyme, lactase,

2
00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.140
which splits milk sugar in two.

3
00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:09.830
Some factories trap the
enzyme inside small gel beads,

4
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.640
and run the milk through.

5
00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:14.970
Yet in beads, the enzyme
can be less active.

6
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:20.710
In one illustrative data set, its peak
is 75 per cent of the free enzyme's.

7
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.740
So why would a factory
lock its enzymes in place,

8
00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.310
when free enzymes work faster?

9
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.540
In this mission, you'll see
how an enzyme can be held on,

10
00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:32.560
or inside, an insoluble support.

11
00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:37.770
Then you'll compare free and fixed
enzymes from 30 to 70 degrees Celsius,

12
00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:39.890
and test what the data show.

13
00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.510
Your mission: find out why a factory
would lock its enzymes in place.
